    "authorAssociation": "NONE",
    "body": "I'm trying to add keymaps in visual mode of the form `s(` which will add surrounding `()`. I tried the direct approach: \r\n```\r\nvim.keymap.set({ \"x\" }, \"s(\", \"sa(\", { desc = \"Surround selection with ()\" })\r\n```\r\nbut it results in simply inserting `a(`.\r\n\r\nI guess I should call the surround function directly, and not via the keymap `sa`, but I didn't find out how to do it from the docs. Any help appreciated.",
    "bodyHTML": "<p dir=\"auto\">I'm trying to add keymaps in visual mode of the form <code class=\"notranslate\">s(</code> which will add surrounding <code class=\"notranslate\">()</code>. I tried the direct approach:</p>\n<div class=\"snippet-clipboard-content notranslate position-relative overflow-auto\" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content=\"vim.keymap.set({ &quot;x&quot; }, &quot;s(&quot;, &quot;sa(&quot;, { desc = &quot;Surround selection with ()&quot; })\"><pre class=\"notranslate\"><code class=\"notranslate\">vim.keymap.set({ \"x\" }, \"s(\", \"sa(\", { desc = \"Surround selection with ()\" })\n</code></pre></div>\n<p dir=\"auto\">but it results in simply inserting <code class=\"notranslate\">a(</code>.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">I guess I should call the surround function directly, and not via the keymap <code class=\"notranslate\">sa</code>, but I didn't find out how to do it from the docs. Any help appreciated.</p>",
    "bodyText": "I'm trying to add keymaps in visual mode of the form s( which will add surrounding (). I tried the direct approach:\nvim.keymap.set({ \"x\" }, \"s(\", \"sa(\", { desc = \"Surround selection with ()\" })\n\nbut it results in simply inserting a(.\nI guess I should call the surround function directly, and not via the keymap sa, but I didn't find out how to do it from the docs. Any help appreciated.",
    "category": {
        "createdAt": "2022-01-02T14:24:09Z",
        "description": "Ask the community for help",
        "emoji": ":pray:",
        "emojiHTML": "<div>🙏</div>",
        "id": "DIC_kwDOFoksYM4CAgqN",
        "isAnswerable": true,
        "name": "Q&A",
        "slug": "q-a",
        "updatedAt": "2022-01-02T14:24:09Z"
    },
    "closed": true,
    "closedAt": "2024-05-13T08:27:44Z",
    "comment_count": 1,
    "comment_data": [
        {
            "author": {
                "avatarUrl": "https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/24854248?u=d09fd974c6720eba3039db6beb2f1c1efecdb51d&v=4",
                "login": "echasnovski",
                "resourcePath": "/echasnovski",
                "url": "https://github.com/echasnovski"
            },
            "authorAssociation": "MEMBER",
            "body": "By default `vim.keymap.set()` treats keys in right hand side as if they have default meaning, i.e. no remap, i.e. \"noremap\".\r\nYou can achieve what you want by adding `remap = true` option. Like this:\r\n\r\n```\r\nvim.keymap.set({ \"x\" }, \"s(\", \"sa(\", { remap = true, desc = \"Surround selection with ()\" })\r\n```\r\n\r\n------\r\n\r\nJust in case: this approach for adding surrounding will work **only** in Visual mode. In Normal mode there needs to be a target (textobject/motion) after `sa`.",
            "bodyHTML": "<p dir=\"auto\">By default <code class=\"notranslate\">vim.keymap.set()</code> treats keys in right hand side as if they have default meaning, i.e. no remap, i.e. \"noremap\".<br>\nYou can achieve what you want by adding <code class=\"notranslate\">remap = true</code> option. Like this:</p>\n<div class=\"snippet-clipboard-content notranslate position-relative overflow-auto\" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content=\"vim.keymap.set({ &quot;x&quot; }, &quot;s(&quot;, &quot;sa(&quot;, { remap = true, desc = &quot;Surround selection with ()&quot; })\"><pre class=\"notranslate\"><code class=\"notranslate\">vim.keymap.set({ \"x\" }, \"s(\", \"sa(\", { remap = true, desc = \"Surround selection with ()\" })\n</code></pre></div>\n<hr>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Just in case: this approach for adding surrounding will work <strong>only</strong> in Visual mode. In Normal mode there needs to be a target (textobject/motion) after <code class=\"notranslate\">sa</code>.</p>",
            "bodyText": "By default vim.keymap.set() treats keys in right hand side as if they have default meaning, i.e. no remap, i.e. \"noremap\".\nYou can achieve what you want by adding remap = true option. Like this:\nvim.keymap.set({ \"x\" }, \"s(\", \"sa(\", { remap = true, desc = \"Surround selection with ()\" })\n\n\nJust in case: this approach for adding surrounding will work only in Visual mode. In Normal mode there needs to be a target (textobject/motion) after sa.",
